\ Division: `/` vs `div`
\
\ `/` produces exact ratios when integer division is inexact and the
\ ratio library is loaded. `div` always truncates toward zero.
\ When the ratio library is not loaded, `/` also truncates.

use "ratio" ;
use "testing" ;

\ --- Exact division returns an integer either way ---

6 2 / 3 "/ on exact division" assert=
6 2 div 3 "div on exact division" assert=

\ --- Inexact division: `/` preserves precision ---

7 2 / ratio? "/ produces a ratio" assert-true
7 2 / >string "7/2" "ratio display" assert=

7 2 div 3 "div truncates toward zero" assert=

\ --- Ratios recover the original value under multiplication ---

1 3 / 3 * 1 "1/3 * 3 is exactly 1" assert=

\ --- Choose `div` when you need an integer quotient ---
\
\ Time calculations, index math, and similar code that expects an
\ integer result should use `div` so the result stays integral
\ regardless of whether the ratio library is loaded.

100 3 div 33 "integer quotient" assert=
